        "statement": "For the finite-dimensional $C^1$ transition sequences on a fixed strict branch defined in [[alg-checkpointed-reverse-schedules]], provided every schedule replay remains in the declared domains, both schedules return the same terminal value and initial-state cotangent as an uncheckpointed reverse sweep. In uniform-block mode, if $T,m\\ge1$, $q=\\lceil T/m\\rceil$, and the block lengths are $r_1,\\ldots,r_s$, the number of additional unrecorded forward evaluations is exactly $\\sum_{\\ell=1}^s r_\\ell(r_\\ell-1)/2$, at most $m q(q-1)/2$. At most $m$ checkpoint states are saved, with one live replay state and one local step tape. In binomial mode, $C(d,t)=\\binom{d+t}{d}$; any $T\\le C(d,t)$ steps can be reversed with at most $d$ saved snapshots, one recorded forward evaluation and one reverse evaluation per step, at most $t$ additional unrecorded forward evaluations per step, total work at most $\\widehat W+\\overline W+tW$, and storage $G+(d+1)R_s$. Here $W$ is the total cost of one unrecorded full forward pass, $\\widehat W$ the sum of recorded one-step forward costs, $\\overline W$ the sum of local reverse costs, $R_s$ bounds scalar storage for one primal state, and $G$ bounds the largest one-step tape and local reverse scratch, the constant number of live cotangent/work vectors, and scalar recursion-frame records, excluding saved states and one live primal state.",
