        ],
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "statement": "The exact AD identities refer to the real-arithmetic graph and its exact primitive derivatives. A floating execution uses rounded primal values and derivative arithmetic; its returned AD quantity is not asserted to be the ordinary derivative of the end-to-end rounded machine map. The theorem [[thm-graph-gradient-error-from-local-jacobian-perturbations]] propagates specified local Jacobian discrepancies with exact accumulation and excludes rounding in reverse accumulation additions.\n\nFor a concrete mathematical solve primitive, [[lem-certified-linear-solve-jacobian-and-pullback-error]] supplies those discrepancies. With stored primal $\\widehat x$, fixed approximate inverse $B$ assembled from basis solves, certified $\\|I-AB\\|_2\\le d<1$, $\\|b-A\\widehat x\\|_2\\le\\rho$, $\\|B\\|_2\\le M$, and $K=M/(1-d)$, the vector and matrix edges have $\\eta_b=Kd$ and $\\eta_A=Kd\\|\\widehat x\\|+K^2\\rho$. Its additional explicit dot/outer-product allowances cover rounded pullback evaluation. Triangular, successful Cholesky, and QR backends are admissible once those residuals are certified; a small factorization residual alone is insufficient. The lemma includes the derivative-evaluation-point error, conditioning, arithmetic success and replay hypotheses. At the implicit example's $A(0)=I$, the stored exact solves give $B=I$, $\\widehat x=(2,1)^T$, $d=\\rho=0$, and every operation in that particular adjoint evaluation is exact, so its derivative is exactly $-1$.\n\nA Cholesky or QR factor-output primitive is a different smooth map from the mathematical solve. Its derivative must be evaluated on one fixed strict branch, with its own local derivative errors, or its finite loop must be expanded into the scalar primitives of the graph. The solve bound does not identify a factorization residual with a factor-output derivative bound.",
        "strategy": "The exact AD identities refer to the real-arithmetic graph and its exact primitive derivatives. A floating execution uses rounded primal values and derivative arithmetic; its returned AD quantity is not asserted to be the ordinary derivative of the end-to-end rounded machine map. The theorem [[thm-graph-gradient-error-from-local-jacobian-perturbations]] propagates specified local Jacobian discrepancies with exact accumulation and excludes rounding in reverse accumulation additions.\n\nFor a concrete mathematical solve primitive, [[lem-certified-linear-solve-jacobian-and-pullback-error]] supplies those discrepancies. With stored primal $\\widehat x$, fixed approximate inverse $B$ assembled from basis solves, certified $\\|I-AB\\|_2\\le d<1$, $\\|b-A\\widehat x\\|_2\\le\\rho$, $\\|B\\|_2\\le M$, and $K=M/(1-d)$, the vector and matrix edges have $\\eta_b=Kd$ and $\\eta_A=Kd\\|\\widehat x\\|+K^2\\rho$. Its additional explicit dot/outer-product allowances cover rounded pullback evaluation. Triangular, successful Cholesky, and QR backends are admissible once those residuals are certified; a small factorization residual alone is insufficient. The lemma includes the derivative-evaluation-point error, conditioning, arithmetic success and replay hypotheses. At the implicit example's $A(0)=I$, the stored exact solves give $B=I$, $\\widehat x=(2,1)^T$, $d=\\rho=0$, and every operation in that particular adjoint evaluation is exact, so its derivative is exactly $-1$.\n\nA Cholesky or QR factor-output primitive is a different smooth map from the mathematical solve. Its derivative must be evaluated on one fixed strict branch, with its own local derivative errors, or its finite loop must be expanded into the scalar primitives of the graph. The solve bound does not identify a factorization residual with a factor-output derivative bound.",
