      {
        "id": "lem-certified-linear-solve-jacobian-and-pullback-error",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Certified inverse defects and primal residuals bound linear-solve Jacobian and pullback errors",
        "deps": [
          "thm-implicit-differentiation-forward-and-adjoint-formulas",
          "lem-rounded-linear-and-normal-residual-certificates",
          "thm-singular-value-decomposition"
        ],
        "statement": "For the real mathematical solve primitive $\\phi(A,b)=A^{-1}b$, let $A,B\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, $b,\\widehat x\\in\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge1$, and suppose certified exact-residual bounds satisfy\n$$\\|I-AB\\|_2\\le d<1,\\qquad \\|b-A\\widehat x\\|_2\\le\\rho,\\qquad \\|B\\|_2\\le M,\\qquad K=\\frac{M}{1-d}.$$\nThen $A$ is invertible; writing $x=A^{-1}b$, the fixed linear derivative approximations $\\widetilde J_bh=Bh$ and $\\widetilde J_AH=-BH\\widehat x$ obey\n$$\\|\\widetilde J_b-J_b\\|\\le\\eta_b=Kd,\\qquad \\|\\widetilde J_A-J_A\\|\\le\\eta_A=Kd\\|\\widehat x\\|_2+K^2\\rho.$$\nUse Euclidean vector norms and Frobenius matrix-input norm. Their adjoints are $B^Tc$ and $-(B^Tc)\\widehat x^T$. If these adjoints are evaluated by successful length-$n$ rounded real dots and componentwise outer products, put $t=\\gamma_n\\|B\\|_F$, assuming $nu<1$. The resulting cotangent errors are bounded by $(\\eta_b+t)\\|c\\|_2$ and $[\\eta_A+(t+u(M+t))\\|\\widehat x\\|_2]\\|c\\|_2$, respectively. These are derivative approximations to the mathematical solve map, not derivatives of the rounded program.\n\nThe fixed $B$ may be assembled from stored basis-vector solves using a triangular, Cholesky or QR backend; its defect must be certified rather than inferred from a factor residual. Replay must preserve the same $\\widehat x,B$ and strict graph branch. Reverse accumulation additions are excluded.",
        "strategy": "1.1 For every $z$, $\\|ABz\\|\\ge(1-d)\\|z\\|$. Thus [F3] makes $AB$, hence both square factors, invertible and gives $\\|(AB)^{-1}\\|\\le1/(1-d)$. Since $A^{-1}=B(AB)^{-1}$, $\\|A^{-1}\\|\\le K$. The identity $B-A^{-1}=A^{-1}(AB-I)$ gives $\\|B-A^{-1}\\|\\le Kd$, while $\\widehat x-x=A^{-1}(A\\widehat x-b)$ gives $\\|\\widehat x-x\\|\\le K\\rho$. The residuals in these identities are exact; [F2] explains how to certify them from rounded evaluations. [given, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n1.2 Apply [F1] at the exact point $(A,b)$. For the matrix edge, $BH\\widehat x-A^{-1}Hx=(B-A^{-1})H\\widehat x+A^{-1}H(\\widehat x-x)$. Since $\\|H\\|_2\\le\\|H\\|_F$, step 1.1 gives $\\eta_A$; the vector edge gives $\\eta_b$. Taking Euclidean/Frobenius adjoints yields the stated pullbacks with the same norm discrepancies. This explicitly accounts for the perturbed primal evaluation point through $\\rho$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Real dot-product coefficient expansion bounds $\\|\\widehat\\lambda-B^Tc\\|_2\\le t\\|c\\|_2$, hence $\\|\\widehat\\lambda\\|_2\\le(M+t)\\|c\\|_2$. Each rounded outer-product entry has error at most $u|\\widehat\\lambda_i\\widehat x_j|$, so its Frobenius error is at most $u(M+t)\\|\\widehat x\\|_2\\|c\\|_2$. Combine these with step 1.2 and the contribution $t\\|\\widehat x\\|_2\\|c\\|_2$ from the rounded adjoint solve. [given, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n2.2 For each realized nonzero $c$, any cotangent error vector $z$ is the action on $c$ of the linear map $zc^T/\\|c\\|^2$, whose induced norm is $\\|z\\|/\\|c\\|$; vectorize the matrix cotangent for the matrix edge. When $c=0$, successful operations return exact zero. Thus the realized rounded pullbacks admit local linear-map perturbations with the displayed bounds, without asserting global linearity of rounding. The graph perturbation theorem may propagate these realized maps with exact accumulation. [step 2.1, algebra, construct] ∎",
        "sources": [
