        "dependency_level": 3,
        "statement": "Let $A(\\theta)=\\begin{bmatrix}1&\\theta\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=(2,1)^T$, and define $u(\\theta)$ by $A(\\theta)u(\\theta)=b$. For the scalar objective $J(\\theta)=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\end{bmatrix}u(\\theta)$, at $\\theta=0$ the exact derivative and the adjoint formula both equal $-1$.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct solve and adjoint substitution.\n\n1.1 The system gives $u_2=1$ and $u_1+\\theta u_2=2$, hence $u(\\theta)=(2-\\theta,1)^T$ and $J(\\theta)=3-\\theta$. Therefore $J'(0)=-1$. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Write $R(u,\\theta)=A(\\theta)u-b$. At $\\theta=0$, $u=(2,1)^T$, $R_u=A(0)=I$, $R_\\theta=A_\\theta u=(1,0)^T$, and $J_u=(1,1)$. The adjoint equation $R_u^T\\lambda=J_u^T$ gives $\\lambda=(1,1)^T$. [given, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The objective has no explicit $\\theta$ dependence, so $J_\\theta=0$; the adjoint formula gives $J_\\theta-\\lambda^TR_\\theta=-(1,1)\\cdot(1,0)=-1$. [F1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The adjoint result agrees with the direct derivative in step 1.1, verifying this nonsingular linear-solve instance. [step 1.1, step 3.1]\n\n4.2 In any arithmetic satisfying the operationwise model and representing $0,1,2,3$, with these small operations in range, at $\\theta=0$ the primal and basis solves give $\\widehat x=(2,1)^T$ and $B=I$ exactly. Their inverse defect and primal residual are zero. The certified solve-Jacobian result [[lem-certified-linear-solve-jacobian-and-pullback-error]] therefore gives zero fixed-map discrepancy; the actual dot and product operations here are also exact, and return derivative $-1$. [F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
