      {
        "id": "def-complete-ordered-real-field",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Dedekind-complete ordered real field",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Steven G. Krantz, Foundations of Analysis, §1.1, Theorems 1.7 and 1.10",
              "url": "https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9781482220759_A37871115/preview-9781482220759_A37871115.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "A **Dedekind-complete ordered field** is a commutative field $\\mathbb R$ with\nidentity $1>0$, a total order compatible with addition and multiplication, and\nthe least-upper-bound axiom: every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb R$ that is\nbounded above has a least upper bound in $\\mathbb R$. Throughout this batch,\nall real variables, intervals, limits, derivatives and integrals are taken in\nsuch a field. The integers and rationals are interpreted in their usual\nembedded copies. For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define $|x|=x$ when $x\\ge0$ and\n$|x|=-x$ when $x<0$."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-continuity-and-derivative-on-real-interval",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Continuity and derivative on a real interval",
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-real-field"
        ],
        "statement": "For $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$, continuity at $x\\in[a,b]$ means that for every\n$\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that\n$|f(t)-f(x)|<\\varepsilon$ whenever $t\\in[a,b]$ and $|t-x|<\\delta$.\nDifferentiability at an interior point $c$ means that the two-sided limit\n$$\nf'(c)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(c+h)-f(c)}{h}\n$$\nexists in the real field; differentiability on $(a,b)$ means this holds at\nevery interior point. For higher derivatives, define recursively\n$f^{(j+1)}=(f^{(j)})'$ on the points where the derivative exists. The class\n$C^{n+1}$ on an open interval means that these derivatives through order\n$n+1$ exist and are continuous there.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Michael C. Colding, MIT 18.100B Real Analysis, Lecture 15: Derivatives; Laws for Differentiation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec15.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Michael C. Colding, MIT 18.100B Real Analysis, Lecture 16",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec16.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Dyadic floating-point format and directed rounding",
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-real-field"
        ],
        "statement": "An exact dyadic rational is a number $m2^q$ with integers $m,q$.\nFix integers $p\\ge2$ and $e_{\\min}\\le e_{\\max}$. Put\n$\\eta=2^{e_{\\min}-p+1}$ and\n$N_{\\max}=(2^p-1)2^{e_{\\max}-p+1}$. The finite representable set $\\mathbb F$\nconsists of zero, both signs of every normal number $k2^{e-p+1}$ with\n$e_{\\min}\\le e\\le e_{\\max}$ and $2^{p-1}\\le k<2^p$, and both signs of every\nsubnormal number $k\\eta$ with $1\\le k<2^{p-1}$. The smallest positive\nsubnormal is $\\eta$, the smallest positive normal is $2^{e_{\\min}}$, and the\nlargest finite magnitude is $N_{\\max}$. The extended endpoint set also contains\n$-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$ as enclosure sentinels; they are not finite machine\nnumbers.\n\nFor a real $x$ with $|x|\\le N_{\\max}$, define $\\operatorname{rd}(x)$ as the\ngreatest member of $\\mathbb F$ not exceeding $x$, and $\\operatorname{ru}(x)$ as\nthe least member of $\\mathbb F$ not less than $x$. Define\n$\\rho(x)=\\operatorname{ru}(x)-\\operatorname{rd}(x)$. For nonzero $x$ in the\nsubnormal range, define the absolute-error bound $\\epsilon_{\\rm abs}(x)=\\eta$;\nfor $2^e\\le |x|<2^{e+1}$ in the normal range, define\n$\\epsilon_{\\rm abs}(x)=2^{e-p+2}$. At finite overflow, a directed result is\nthe corresponding infinite sentinel and its error bound is $+\\infty$; the\nstatus is `overflow`. The rounding procedure reports `finite`, `subnormal`, or\n`overflow`, so no relative-error assumption is implicit.\n\nAn exact rational intermediate may be converted by comparing integer\nnumerators and denominators after clearing powers of two. A directed interval\nendpoint is the resulting greatest lower or least upper member of $\\mathbb F$.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Tucker, Validated Numerics: A Short Introduction to Rigorous Computations, Ch. 1 Computer Arithmetic",
              "url": "https://pup-assets.imgix.net/onix/images/9780691247656/9780691247656.pdf?fm=pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rump, Verification methods: Rigorous results using floating-point arithmetic, §4",
              "url": "https://www.tuhh.de/ti3/rump/intlab/ActaNumerica2010.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-archimedean-property-from-completeness",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Dedekind completeness implies the Archimedean property",
        "deps": [
