      {
        "id": "alg-dyadic-square-root-bisection",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Exact dyadic bisection for square-root endpoints",
        "deps": [
          "def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding",
          "lem-nonnegative-square-root-exists-and-unique"
        ],
        "statement": "Input: a nonnegative exact dyadic rational $x$ and integer request $n\\ge1$.\nReturn exact dyadic bounds for $\\sqrt{x}$ as follows.\n\n1. If $x=0$, return $[0,0]$.\n2. Otherwise determine the unique integer $j$ with $2^j\\le x<2^{j+1}$ from the\n   exact dyadic representation, put $m=\\lfloor j/2\\rfloor$ and\n   $\\delta=2^{m-n}$.\n3. Set integer indices $L=0$ and $U=2^{n+1}$. While $U-L>1$, put\n   $Q=\\lfloor(L+U)/2\\rfloor$. If $(Q\\delta)^2\\le x$, replace $L$ by $Q$;\n   otherwise replace $U$ by $Q$.\n4. Return $[L\\delta,U\\delta]$. To store the endpoints in a selected finite\n   format, round the lower endpoint down and the upper endpoint up, reporting\n   `subnormal` or `overflow` as required.\n\nAll comparisons and multiplications in the loop are exact integer/dyadic\noperations. The loop has at most $n+1$ iterations.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-dyadic-square-root-bisection-encloses"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Kearfott, Interval Computations: Introduction, Uses, and Resources, §1",
              "url": "https://interval.louisiana.edu/preprints/survey.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rump, Verification methods: Rigorous results using floating-point arithmetic, §7",
              "url": "https://www.tuhh.de/ti3/rump/intlab/ActaNumerica2010.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Real intervals, complex rectangles and ball enclosures",
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-real-field",
          "lem-nonnegative-square-root-exists-and-unique"
        ],
        "statement": "A **real interval** is a closed set $[a,b]\\subset\\mathbb R$ with finite dyadic\nendpoints $a\\le b$. Its width is $b-a$, midpoint is $(a+b)/2$, and its\nmidpoint-radius form is $m+[-r,r]$ with $m=(a+b)/2$ and $r=(b-a)/2$. The empty\nset is the status `empty`; the whole real line is `entire`. `overflow`,\n`domain-error`, `split-domain`, and `inconclusive` are computation statuses,\nnot finite intervals.\n\nA **complex rectangle** is $X+iY=\\{x+iy:x\\in X,\\ y\\in Y\\}$ for real intervals\n$X,Y$. Complex addition and multiplication use\n$(x+iy)+(u+iv)=(x+u)+i(y+v)$ and\n$(x+iy)(u+iv)=(xu-yv)+i(xv+yu)$. For $z=x+iy$, its complex modulus is\n$|z|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, and for $z\\ne0$ its reciprocal is\n$1/z=(x-iy)/(x^2+y^2)$. A\n**complex ball** $B(c,r)$ is\n$\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z-c|\\le r\\}$ with dyadic complex center $c$ and dyadic\n$r\\ge0$. For any two represented sets $S,T$, the inclusion order is\n$S\\sqsubseteq T$ iff $S\\subseteq T$.\n\nThe width of a complex rectangle with coordinate widths $w_X,w_Y$ is its\nEuclidean diameter $\\sqrt{w_X^2+w_Y^2}$; the width of a ball $B(c,r)$ is its\ndiameter $2r$. Width comparisons against a dyadic tolerance can be decided by\nexact rational comparisons after squaring the nonnegative quantities.\n\nThe **range** of a real or complex function $f$ on a set $S$ is\n$f(S)=\\{f(x):x\\in S\\cap\\operatorname{dom}(f)\\}$. A finite expression DAG is a\nfinite directed acyclic graph with input nodes, constant nodes, and operation\nnodes; constants are exact dyadics or are supplied with certified enclosing\nintervals. Sharing a node means the exact same intermediate value is reused.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Kearfott, Interval Computations: Introduction, Uses, and Resources, §§1–2",
              "url": "https://interval.louisiana.edu/preprints/survey.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rump, Verification methods: Rigorous results using floating-point arithmetic, §5.1",
              "url": "https://www.tuhh.de/ti3/rump/intlab/ActaNumerica2010.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-directed-dyadic-rounding-enclosure-and-error",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Directed dyadic rounding encloses with explicit underflow and overflow bounds",
        "deps": [
          "def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding"
        ],
        "statement": "For finite $x$ with $|x|\\le N_{\\max}$, the directed roundings defined in\n[[def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding]] satisfy\n$\\operatorname{rd}(x)\\le x\\le\\operatorname{ru}(x)$ and each endpoint error is\nat most the stated $\\epsilon_{\\rm abs}(x)$ (with zero error when $x$ is\nrepresentable). For $x>N_{\\max}$ the pair $(N_{\\max},+\\infty)$ encloses $x$;\nfor $x<-N_{\\max}$ the pair $(-\\infty,-N_{\\max})$ encloses $x$. Overflow has\nerror bound $+\\infty$ and cannot be reported as a finite-error result.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Tucker, Validated Numerics: A Short Introduction to Rigorous Computations, Ch. 1 §§1.2–1.5",
              "url": "https://pup-assets.imgix.net/onix/images/9780691247656/9780691247656.pdf?fm=pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rump, Verification methods: Rigorous results using floating-point arithmetic, §§4–5.3",
              "url": "https://www.tuhh.de/ti3/rump/intlab/ActaNumerica2010.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-outward-rounded-interval-and-ball-operations",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Outward-rounded interval, rectangle and ball operations",
        "deps": [
          "def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding",
          "def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls",
          "alg-dyadic-square-root-bisection"
        ],
        "statement": "For real intervals $X=[a,b]$ and $Y=[c,d]$, compute the displayed exact\nendpoint formulas and round the lower endpoint down and the upper endpoint up\nin the format of\n[[def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding]]. If either exact\nendpoint overflows, return `overflow` with the outward extended endpoints;\nnever substitute a finite endpoint for an overflow sentinel. Addition is\n$[a+c,b+d]$, subtraction is $[a-d,b-c]$, and multiplication is\n$[\\min(ac,ad,bc,bd),\\max(ac,ad,bc,bd)]$. Division is defined only when\n$0\\notin Y$ and is $X[1/d,1/c]$, with reciprocal endpoints computed as exact\nrationals before outward rounding. Squaring is\n$[0,\\max(a^2,b^2)]$ when $0\\in X$ and\n$[\\min(a^2,b^2),\\max(a^2,b^2)]$ otherwise. Square root is defined only for\n$X\\subseteq[0,\\infty)$ and is enclosed by applying\n[[alg-dyadic-square-root-bisection]] to the exact dyadic endpoints with\nrequest $n=p$, where $p$ is the selected format precision, and rounding\noutward for storage.\n\nIf $0\\in Y$ for division, return `split-domain` with the two subdomains\n$Y\\cap(-\\infty,0)$ and $Y\\cap(0,\\infty)$, or `entire` when the caller does not\nrequest subdivision. No finite quotient interval is returned in this case.\nAn invalid square-root domain returns `domain-error` or an explicitly\nrestricted nonnegative subdomain.\n\nComplex-rectangle addition, subtraction and multiplication use\n$(x+iy)(u+iv)=(xu-yv)+i(xv+yu)$, applying the real interval operations to\neach component. For a rectangle $X+iY$ excluding $0$, define its reciprocal by\n$(X-iY)/(X^2+Y^2)$; the denominator interval is strictly positive because at\nleast one coordinate interval excludes zero. Division multiplies by this\nreciprocal.\n\nFor a ball $B(c,r)$, let $X_B=[\\Re c-r,\\Re c+r]$ and\n$Y_B=[\\Im c-r,\\Im c+r]$ be its coordinate projection intervals. For a real\ninterval $J$, let $M(J)=\\max(|\\inf J|,|\\sup J|)$ and let $d(J)=0$ if\n$0\\in J$, otherwise $d(J)=\\min(|\\inf J|,|\\sup J|)$. Put\n$M_B=M(X_B)+M(Y_B)$ and $m_B=\\max(d(X_B),d(Y_B))$. Then\n$|z|\\le M_B$ for every $z\\in B(c,r)$ and $|z|\\ge m_B$ for every such $z$.\nDefine ball addition and subtraction as\n$B(0,\\operatorname{ru}(M_{B_1}+M_{B_2}))$, multiplication as\n$B(0,\\operatorname{ru}(M_{B_1}M_{B_2}))$, and division as\n$B(0,\\operatorname{ru}(M_{B_1}/m_{B_2}))$ only when $m_{B_2}>0$. These\norigin-centered output balls are conservative but inclusion-isotone. If\n$m_{B_2}=0$, return `split-domain`, `entire`, or `inconclusive`; if a radius\noverlows, return `overflow`.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-outward-rounded-operations-are-isotone-and-dag-sound"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Kearfott, Interval Computations: Introduction, Uses, and Resources, §1",
              "url": "https://interval.louisiana.edu/preprints/survey.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rump, Verification methods: Rigorous results using floating-point arithmetic, §§5.1–5.5",
              "url": "https://www.tuhh.de/ti3/rump/intlab/ActaNumerica2010.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-real-and-complex-modulus-rules",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Real and complex modulus product and triangle inequalities",
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-real-field",
          "def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls",
          "lem-nonnegative-square-root-exists-and-unique"
        ],
        "statement": "For real $x,y$, $|xy|=|x||y|$ and $|x+y|\\le|x|+|y|$. For complex\n$z,w$, $|zw|=|z||w|$, $|z+w|\\le|z|+|w|$, and if $w\\ne0$ then\n$|z/w|=|z|/|w|$.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
