      {
        "id": "thm-strict-row-diagonal-dominance-implies-jacobi-and-gauss-seidel-convergence",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Strict row diagonal dominance guarantees Jacobi and Gauss–Seidel convergence",
        "deps": [
          "def-diagonal-lower-upper-splitting-convention",
          "alg-jacobi-stationary-iteration",
          "alg-gauss-seidel-stationary-iteration",
          "def-stationary-iteration-from-a-matrix-splitting",
          "thm-stationary-splitting-converges-iff-the-iteration-matrix-has-spectral-radius-below-one",
          "thm-forward-and-back-substitution-are-correct-unique-and-quadratic-cost"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ and $A=(a_{ij})\\in M_n(\\mathbb R)$ satisfy strict row diagonal\ndominance:\n\n$$|a_{ii}|>\\sum_{j\\ne i}|a_{ij}|\\qquad(i=1,\\ldots,n).$$\n\nThen the Jacobi and fixed-order Gauss–Seidel iterations defined by\n[[alg-jacobi-stationary-iteration]] and\n[[alg-gauss-seidel-stationary-iteration]] converge in exact arithmetic to the\nunique solution of $Ax=b$ for every $b,x^{(0)}\\in\\mathbb R^n$.\n\n",
        "strategy": "1.1 By [A1], every $a_{ii}\\ne0$. Thus $D$ and the lower-triangular matrix $D-L$ have nonzero diagonal, so the solves in [F1] and [F2] are uniquely defined by [L2]. [A1, F1, F2, L2, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [F1]–[F2] and [L3], the iteration matrices are $G_J=D^{-1}(L+U)$ and $G_G=(D-L)^{-1}U$. [F1, F2, L3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For each row $i$, set $q_i=\\sum_{j\\ne i}|a_{ij}|/|a_{ii}|$ and $q_J=\\max_i q_i<1$. Since $L_{ij}=-a_{ij}$ below the diagonal and $U_{ij}=-a_{ij}$ above it, $\\sum_j|(G_J)_{ij}|=q_i$. Hence for any eigenpair $G_Jz=\\lambda z$ with $z\\ne0$, choose $i$ with $|z_i|=\\max_j|z_j|$; the row equation gives $|\\lambda||z_i|\\le q_i|z_i|\\le q_J|z_i|$, so $|\\lambda|<1$ and $\\rho(G_J)<1$. [A1, L1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.2 Let $G_Gz=\\lambda z$ with $z\\ne0$, and choose $i$ with $|z_i|=\\max_j|z_j|=:R$. The equation $Uz=\\lambda(D-L)z$ in row $i$ is $\\sum_{j>i}U_{ij}z_j=\\lambda(a_{ii}z_i-\\sum_{j<i}L_{ij}z_j)$, so $|\\lambda|\\,|a_{ii}z_i-\\sum_{j<i}L_{ij}z_j| \\le\\sum_{j>i}|U_{ij}||z_j|$. By the reverse triangle inequality and [A1], the left factor is at least $|\\lambda|(|a_{ii}|-\\sum_{j<i}|a_{ij}|)R$, while the right side is at most $\\sum_{j>i}|a_{ij}|R$. Therefore $|\\lambda|\\le\\max_i\\frac{\\sum_{j>i}|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|-\\sum_{j<i}|a_{ij}|}<1$; the denominators are positive and each strict inequality follows from [A1]. Thus $\\rho(G_G)<1$. [A1, L1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The bounds in steps 3.1 and 3.2 and [L4] imply that both methods converge to the unique solution of $Ax=b$ for every $b$ and every initial vector. [L4, step 3.1, step 3.2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
