      },
      {
        "id": "thm-sor-converges-iff-a-is-positive-definite-and-zero-less-than-omega-less-than-two",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "SOR converges exactly for positive-definite A and relaxation parameter in (0, 2)",
        "deps": [
          "def-diagonal-lower-upper-splitting-convention",
          "alg-sor-stationary-iteration",
          "def-stationary-iteration-from-a-matrix-splitting",
          "thm-stationary-splitting-converges-iff-the-iteration-matrix-has-spectral-radius-below-one",
          "def-cholesky-factorisation-with-positive-diagonal",
          "thm-forward-and-back-substitution-are-correct-unique-and-quadratic-cost",
          "thm-determinant-multiplicative",
          "thm-determinant-of-a-triangular-matrix",
          "thm-determinant-is-product-of-eigenvalues",
          "thm-the-complex-numbers-are-algebraically-closed"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, $A\\in M_n(\\mathbb C)$ be Hermitian with strictly positive\ndiagonal, and let $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Write\n$A=D-L-L^*$ by [[def-diagonal-lower-upper-splitting-convention]], and define\n\n$$M_\\omega=\\omega^{-1}D-L,\\qquad\nN_\\omega=(\\omega^{-1}-1)D+L^*,\\qquad\nG_\\omega=M_\\omega^{-1}N_\\omega.$$\n\nThe SOR iteration is $x^{(k+1)}=G_\\omega x^{(k)}+M_\\omega^{-1}b$. It converges\nto the unique solution of $Ax=b$ for every $b,x^{(0)}\\in\\mathbb C^n$ if and\nonly if $A$ is positive definite (meaning $x^*Ax>0$ for every nonzero $x$)\nand $0<\\omega<2$.\n\n",
        "strategy": "1.1 By [A1] and [F1], $M_\\omega$ is lower triangular with diagonal $a_{ii}/\\omega\\ne0$, hence invertible by [L5]. Also $A=M_\\omega-N_\\omega$ by [L1], so [L2] applies with iteration matrix $G_\\omega$. The matrix $N_\\omega$ is upper triangular with diagonal entries $(\\omega^{-1}-1)a_{ii}$. Thus [L6]–[L7] give $\\det(M_\\omega)=\\omega^{-n}\\prod_i a_{ii}$ and $\\det(N_\\omega)=(\\omega^{-1}-1)^n\\prod_i a_{ii}$. From $M_\\omega G_\\omega=N_\\omega$ and multiplicativity, $\\det(G_\\omega)=\\det(N_\\omega)/\\det(M_\\omega)=(1-\\omega)^n$. [A1, F1, L1, L2, L5, L6, L7, algebra]\n\n1.2 For sufficiency, suppose $A$ is positive definite and $0<\\omega<2$. Then $Q:=M_\\omega+M_\\omega^*-A=(2/\\omega-1)D$ is positive definite. For an eigenpair $G_\\omega v=\\lambda v$ with $v\\ne0$, one has $\\lambda\\ne1$ since $Av=(1-\\lambda)M_\\omega v$ and $A$ is invertible. Put $a=v^*Av>0$. Then $v^*M_\\omega v=a/(1-\\lambda)$, so $v^*Qv=a\\left(2\\operatorname{Re}\\frac{1}{1-\\lambda}-1\\right) =a\\frac{1-|\\lambda|^2}{|1-\\lambda|^2}>0$. Therefore $|\\lambda|<1$ for every eigenvalue and $\\rho(G_\\omega)<1$. [A1, F1, L1, L2, L4, algebra]\n\n2.1 If SOR converges for every right-hand side and start, [L3] gives $\\rho(G_\\omega)<1$. By [L8]–[L9], $|\\det(G_\\omega)|=\\prod_{j=1}^n|\\lambda_j|\\le\\rho(G_\\omega)^n<1$. Step 1.1 therefore gives $|1-\\omega|<1$, which for real $\\omega$ is equivalent to $0<\\omega<2$. [L3, L8, L9, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Assume now that SOR converges for every right-hand side and start. Then $\\rho(G_\\omega)<1$ by [L3], so $1$ is not an eigenvalue of $G_\\omega$ and $I-G_\\omega$ is invertible. By [L2], $A=M_\\omega(I-G_\\omega)$ is invertible. For $b=0$, its unique solution is $x_*=0$. For arbitrary $e^{(0)}:=x^{(0)}$, set $e^{(k)}:=x^{(k)}-x_*=x^{(k)}$ and $\\delta_k=e^{(k+1)}-e^{(k)}$. The homogeneous SOR recurrence gives $M_\\omega e^{(k+1)}=N_\\omega e^{(k)}$, hence $Ae^{(k)}+M_\\omega\\delta_k=0$. Expanding the Hermitian quadratic form gives $(e^{(k)})^*Ae^{(k)}-(e^{(k+1)})^*Ae^{(k+1)} =\\delta_k^*(M_\\omega+M_\\omega^*-A)\\delta_k =(2/\\omega-1)\\delta_k^*D\\delta_k$. The last equality follows from [L1] and [F1]. [A1, F1, L1, L2, L3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 By step 2.1, $c:=2/\\omega-1>0$. Summing the identity in step 2.2 from $k=0$ to $N-1$ and taking $N\\to\\infty$ (the error tends to $0$ by the assumed convergence) gives $(e^{(0)})^*Ae^{(0)}=c\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\delta_k^*D\\delta_k$. Moreover $\\delta_0\\ne0$: otherwise step 2.2 implies $Ae^{(0)}=0$, contrary to invertibility of $A$. Since $D$ has positive diagonal, the first summand is strictly positive and all others are nonnegative. Thus $(e^{(0)})^*Ae^{(0)}>0$ for every nonzero $e^{(0)}$, so [L4] gives that $A$ is positive definite. [A1, L3, L4, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 By [L3], $\\rho(G_\\omega)<1$ implies convergence to the unique solution for every right-hand side and every initial vector. Steps 2.1 and 3.1 prove the reverse implication. [L3, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 1.2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Yousef Saad, Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems, 2nd ed., §4.2.4",
              "url": "https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~saad/IterMethBook_2ndEd.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed p. 122, Theorem 4.10: for symmetric A with positive diagonal and 0<ω<2, SOR converges for every start iff A is positive definite. The local proof below also verifies the Hermitian extension."
