      {
        "id": "lem-finitely-generated-euclidean-cones-are-closed",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A finitely generated cone in Euclidean space is closed",
        "deps": [
          "cor-finite-dimensional-subspaces-are-closed",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "statement": "For any finite list $a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in\\mathbb R^n$, including the empty\nlist, the cone\n$$\nK=\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^m t_j a_j:t_j\\ge0\\right\\}\n$$\nis a closed convex subset of $\\mathbb R^n$.",
        "strategy": "1.1 Every $y\\in K$ has a representation whose positive-coefficient vectors are linearly independent. Start with any nonnegative coefficient list and discard its zero coefficients. If its remaining vectors are dependent, take a nonzero relation $\\sum_j c_j a_j=0$ and change its sign so some $c_j>0$. For the current positive coefficients $t_j$, put $s=\\min_{c_j>0}t_j/c_j$. Replacing each $t_j$ by $t_j-sc_j$ preserves the represented vector, leaves every coefficient nonnegative, and makes at least one positive coefficient zero. Repeating at most $m$ times leaves an independent positive support. [given, choose, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix an independent index set $J$ and let $A_J$ have columns $a_j$, $j\\in J$. Its Gram matrix $G_J=A_J^TA_J$ is invertible: for every nonzero $v$, $v^TG_Jv=\\|A_Jv\\|_2^2>0$ by independence. On the closed subspace $S_J=\\operatorname{span}\\{a_j:j\\in J\\}$, the map $R_J(y)=G_J^{-1}A_J^Ty$ is continuous and satisfies $A_JR_J(y)=y$. Hence $A_J(\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{|J|})= S_J\\cap R_J^{-1}(\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}^{|J|})$ is closed: $S_J$ is closed by [F1], the nonnegative orthant is closed, and $R_J$ is continuous. For $J=\\varnothing$ this cone is $\\{0\\}$, also closed. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 By step 1.1, $K$ is the union of the cones in step 1.2 over independent subsets $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. There are only finitely many such subsets, so this union is closed. Each component is a convex cone, and directly taking nonnegative combinations shows $K$ is convex. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
              "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 12, Section 12.3, printed pp. 339–341; the KKT multiplier proof requires closedness of a finitely generated gradient cone."
            },
            {
              "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
              "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 2, Section 2.5.1, printed pp. 50–51; Euclidean projection and separation for closed convex sets."
            }
