      {
        "id": "def-kkt-residual-and-normal-cone",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Scaled KKT residual and convex normal cone",
        "deps": [
          "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "statement": "Use the program and dimensions of\n[[def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry]]. For any $x\\in U$,\n$\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R^m$, define coordinatewise\n$[v]_i^+=\\max\\{v_i,0\\}$ and the scaled residual\n$$\nr_{\\mathrm{KKT}}(x,\\nu,\\lambda)=\\|\\nabla f(x)+Dh(x)^T\\nu+Dg(x)^T\\lambda\\|_2+\\|h(x)\\|_2+\\|[g(x)]^+\\|_2+\\|[-\\lambda]^+\\|_2+\\|\\lambda\\circ g(x)\\|_2.\n$$\nThe symbol $\\circ$ denotes coordinatewise multiplication. The norm is the\nEuclidean norm from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and the norm of the\nzero-dimensional vector is $0$.\n\nFor a convex set $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $x\\in C$, its (outward) normal\ncone is\n$$\nN_C(x)=\\{v\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle v,y-x\\rangle\\le0\\text{ for every }y\\in C\\}.\n$$\nThis definition applies in particular when $C$ is closed and convex.",
        "strategy": "Definition only; no phase proof is applicable.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
              "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 2.4–2.5 and 5.1, printed pp. 50–60 and 215–224; normal vectors, Lagrangian, and dual function conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
              "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 12, Section 12.2, printed pp. 327–331; KKT residual components and complementarity convention."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-farkas-alternative-for-finite-linear-systems",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Farkas alternative for a finite linear system",
        "deps": [
          "lem-finitely-generated-euclidean-cones-are-closed",
          "thm-nearest-point-in-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set",
          "lem-projection-onto-a-closed-convex-set-has-the-variational-inequality",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}$ and $b\\in\\mathbb R^r$. Exactly one of the\nfollowing holds:\n\n1. There is $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $x\\ge0$ and $Ax=b$.\n2. There is $y\\in\\mathbb R^r$ with $A^Ty\\ge0$ and $b^Ty<0$.\n\nInequalities between vectors are coordinatewise.",
        "strategy": "1.1 Let $C=\\{\\sum_{j=1}^n x_j A_j:x_j\\ge0\\}$, where $A_j$ are the columns of $A$. This is a nonempty convex cone and is closed by [F1]. If $b\\in C$, its nonnegative coefficients give alternative 1. If $b\\notin C$, let $p$ be its nearest point in $C$ by [F2] and put $z=b-p\\ne0$. [F1, F2, given, construct]\n\n2.1 Applying [F3] with $c=0$ and $c=2p$ gives both $\\langle z,p\\rangle\\ge0$ and $\\langle z,p\\rangle\\le0$, hence $\\langle z,p\\rangle=0$. Applying [F3] to arbitrary $c\\in C$ then gives $\\langle z,c\\rangle\\le0$. In particular $A_j^Tz\\le0$ for every column, and $\\langle z,b\\rangle=\\langle z,p+z\\rangle=\\|z\\|_2^2>0$. Thus $y=-z$ satisfies $A^Ty\\ge0$ and $b^Ty=-\\|z\\|_2^2<0$, which is alternative 2. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The alternatives cannot both hold: if $Ax=b$ with $x\\ge0$ and $A^Ty\\ge0$, then $b^Ty=x^TA^Ty\\ge0$, contradicting $b^Ty<0$. Step 1.1 produces one alternative in every case, so exactly one holds. [step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization",
              "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 5, Section 5.8, Farkas' lemma and the theorem of alternatives; the present proof uses Euclidean projection and proves the stated orientation directly."
            },
            {
              "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
              "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 12, Section 12.3, printed pp. 339–341; projection onto a closed gradient cone yields the multiplier alternative."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-mfcq-linearized-cone-equals-bouligand-tangent-cone",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "MFCQ identifies the linearized and Bouligand tangent cones",
        "deps": [
          "def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry",
          "thm-differential-rank-is-lower-semicontinuous",
          "cor-regular-level-set-local-graph-theorem",
          "cor-second-order-taylor-expansion-with-the-hessian"
        ],
        "statement": "For the $C^2$ program of\n[[def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry]], if MFCQ holds at a\nfeasible point $x$, then\n$$T_C(x)=\\mathcal L_C(x).$$",
        "strategy": "1.1 Take $d\\in T_C(x)$ and use its defining feasible sequence $x+t_kd_k$, where $t_k\\downarrow0$ and $d_k\\to d$. For $h(x)=0$, Taylor expansion gives $0=h(x+t_kd_k)=t_kDh(x)d+o(t_k)$, hence $Dh(x)d=0$. For active $i$, feasibility gives $0\\ge g_i(x+t_kd_k)=t_kDg_i(x)d+o(t_k)$, hence $Dg_i(x)d\\le0$. Therefore $d\\in\\mathcal L_C(x)$. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n1.2 Now take $d\\in\\mathcal L_C(x)$. Let $w$ be the MFCQ direction; if no inequality is active set $w=0$. Both $d$ and $w$ lie in $\\ker Dh(x)$. When there are equality constraints, [F2] supplies a local graph $x+u+\\psi(u)$ over this kernel; with no equalities use $\\psi=0$. For $t>0$ small, define $u(t)=td+t^{3/2}w$ and $x(t)=x+u(t)+\\psi(u(t))$. Then $h(x(t))=0$ and [F3] yields $x(t)-x=td+t^{3/2}w+O(t^2)$, so $(x(t)-x)/t\\to d$. [given, F2, F3, construct]\n\n2.1 If $i$ is active, Taylor expansion gives $g_i(x(t))=tDg_i(x)d+t^{3/2}Dg_i(x)w+O(t^2)+o(t^2)$. When $Dg_i(x)d<0$, its order-$t$ term makes this negative for all sufficiently small $t>0$. When $Dg_i(x)d=0$, MFCQ gives $Dg_i(x)w<0$, and its order-$t^{3/2}$ term dominates the remainder of order $t^2$. Inactive inequalities remain strict by continuity. Since there are finitely many inequalities, one positive $\\tau$ works for all of them and every $0<t<\\tau$. Thus $x(t)$ is feasible for such $t$. [step 1.2, given, algebra]\n\n3.1 Taking $t_k=\\tau/2^{k+1}$ gives feasible points with $t_k\\downarrow0$ and $(x(t_k)-x)/t_k\\to d$, so $d\\in T_C(x)$. This proves $\\mathcal L_C(x)\\subseteq T_C(x)$; step 1.1 gives the reverse inclusion. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, construct] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Nocedal and Wright, Numerical Optimization, second edition",
              "url": "https://www.ime.unicamp.br/~pulino/MT404/TextosOnline/NocedalJ.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapter 12, Sections 12.3 and 12.5, printed pp. 337–341 and 351–353; linearized feasible directions, LICQ and MFCQ convention."
