        "statement": "For the $C^2$ program of\n[[def-inequality-constrained-program-and-kkt-geometry]], suppose $x_*$ is\nfeasible and has KKT multipliers $(\\nu,\\lambda)$. If\n$$\nd^T\\nabla_{xx}^2L(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)d>0\\quad\\text{for every nonzero }d\\in\\mathcal K(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda),\n$$\nthen $x_*$ is a strict local minimizer. The condition is vacuous when the\ncritical cone is $\\{0\\}$; no constraint qualification is assumed.",
        "strategy": "1.1 Write $Q(d)=d^T\\nabla_{xx}^2L(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)d$ and $S=\\{d:\\|d\\|_2=1\\}$. The critical cone intersected with $S$ is closed in $S$ by its linear equalities and inequalities. If this intersection is nonempty, it is compact by [F2], and [F2] gives a minimum $\\gamma=\\min Q(d)>0$ there. The relatively open set $N=\\{u\\in S:Q(u)>\\gamma/2\\}$ contains that intersection. If the intersection is empty, put $N=\\varnothing$. [given, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $k\\ge1$, let $D_k$ be the set of unit directions $u=(x-x_*)/\\|x-x_*\\|_2$ for feasible $x\\ne x_*$ with $\\|x-x_*\\|_2<1/k$ and $f(x)\\le f(x_*)$. If $D_k$ is empty for some $k$, there is no such point in that neighborhood and strict local minimality holds. Otherwise, if bad points occur arbitrarily close with directions outside $N$, the nonempty nested closed sets $\\overline{D_k\\setminus N}$ have the finite-intersection property in the compact sphere $S$. By [F3] choose $d\\in\\bigcap_k\\overline{D_k\\setminus N}$; since $N$ is relatively open, $d\\notin N$ and $\\|d\\|_2=1$. [F2, F3, construct]\n\n2.1 For each $k$, the closure property in step 1.2 supplies a direction $u$ with $\\|u-d\\|_2<1/k$ from a feasible bad point $x=x_*+tu$, where $0<t<1/k$ and $f(x)\\le f(x_*)$. Taylor expansion [F4] and equality feasibility imply $Dh(x_*)u=O(t)$; active inequality feasibility gives $Dg_i(x_*)u\\le O(t)$ for every active $i$. The objective inequality gives $\\nabla f(x_*)^Tu\\le O(t)$. Letting $k$ grow in these bounds, using $\\|u-d\\|_2<1/k$ and $t<1/k$, shows $Dh(x_*)d=0$, $Dg_i(x_*)d\\le0$ on the active set, and $\\nabla f(x_*)^Td\\le0$. KKT stationarity and [F1] also give $\\nabla f(x_*)^Td\\ge0$, so $d$ is a unit critical direction. This contradicts $d\\notin N$: if the critical unit set is empty there is no such $d$, and otherwise every such $d$ belongs to $N$. Hence for some $k_0$, every feasible bad point with $0<\\|x-x_*\\|_2<1/k_0$ has direction in $N$. [F1, F3, F4, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 If the critical unit set is empty, step 2.1 already excludes all bad points sufficiently near $x_*$, proving strict local minimality. Otherwise, let $\\gamma>0$ be as in step 1.1. At a feasible $x=x_*+tu$, KKT gives $L(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)=f(x_*)$, $\\nabla_xL(x_*,\\nu,\\lambda)=0$, and $L(x,\\nu,\\lambda)\\le f(x)$. For bad points sufficiently close, step 2.1 gives $u\\in N$, so $Q(u)>\\gamma/2$. The Taylor expansion [F4] of the $C^2$ Lagrangian has a remainder $o(t^2)$, uniformly as $x\\to x_*$; after shrinking the neighborhood it yields $L(x,\\nu,\\lambda)>f(x_*)$. This contradicts $L(x,\\nu,\\lambda)\\le f(x)\\le f(x_*)$. Therefore no distinct feasible point sufficiently near $x_*$ has objective at most $f(x_*)$, proving that $x_*$ is a strict local minimizer. [F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
