        "strategy": "1.1 Put the equality-gradient rows and all active inequality-gradient rows into a matrix $M$. By [F1], its rows are independent. If $M$ has no rows, take $w=0$. Otherwise $MM^T$ is invertible: for nonzero $\\eta$, $\\eta^TMM^T\\eta=\\|M^T\\eta\\|_2^2>0$ by row independence. For the target vector $r$ whose equality entries are $0$ and whose active inequality entries are $-1$, set $w=M^T(MM^T)^{-1}r$. Then $Mw=r$, so $Dh(x)w=0$ and $Dg_i(x)w=-1<0$ for every active $i$. This is MFCQ. [F1, given, construct, algebra]\n\n2.1 If $x$ is a local minimizer, [F2] now supplies at least one KKT pair $(\\nu,\\lambda)$. [F2, step 1.1, given]\n\n3.1 Suppose $(\\nu,\\lambda)$ and $(\\widehat\\nu,\\widehat\\lambda)$ are two KKT pairs at $x$. Complementarity and strict negativity of each inactive $g_i(x)$ force both corresponding multipliers to be $0$. Subtracting the two stationarity equations leaves a linear combination of the equality gradients and active inequality gradients equal to $0$. LICQ makes every coefficient zero, hence $\\nu=\\widehat\\nu$ and $\\lambda=\\widehat\\lambda$. [F1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
